@node Where the result goes
@section Where the result goes

@cindex output actions
@cindex preview buffer

@samp{latex-parser-skill-output-action} decides what happens to the result of a region or buffer command.

@float Table,orgf76dcb0
@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Value
@tab Behavior
@item @samp{preview}
@tab Default. Shows the result for review before anything is replaced
@item @samp{replace}
@tab Replaces the source text in one undo step
@item @samp{insert-after}
@tab Inserts the result after the source text
@item @samp{new-buffer}
@tab Opens the result in a new buffer
@item @samp{file}
@tab Writes @samp{NAME_parsed.tex} beside the buffer's file
@end multitable
@caption{Output actions.}
@end float

The file command follows @samp{latex-parser-skill-file-output-action}, whose default is @samp{file}.
@samp{latex-parser-skill-overwrite} decides what happens when the output file exists.

The package remembers the source text.
When the source changed during the request, it refuses to replace it and opens the result in a new buffer.

@node Programmatic and batch use
@chapter Programmatic and batch use

@cindex batch use
@cindex audio workflow

Other code can call the pipeline directly.
This suits an audio-to-@LaTeX{} workflow that already produces a transcript file.

@lisp
(latex-parser-skill-process-string
 text
 (lambda (latex violations) (insert latex))
 :backend 'claude-code
 :errback (lambda (msg) (message "Failed: %s" msg)))
@end lisp

@samp{latex-parser-skill-process-string-sync} waits for the result and returns a cons of the fragment and the violations.
@samp{latex-parser-skill-finished-hook} runs after every delivery with the same two values.
From the shell, the batch entry point writes @samp{NAME_parsed.tex} for each file.

@example
emacs --batch -L ~/6112MooersLabGitHubLabRepos/latex-parser-skill-el \
      -l latex-parser-skill -f latex-parser-skill-batch notes.txt
@end example

@node Privacy
@chapter Privacy

@cindex privacy

With @samp{latex-parser-skill-confirm-remote} on, the package asks once per session before a backend sends text off the computer.
A request larger than @samp{latex-parser-skill-warn-size} characters asks first too.
Use a local model for unpublished data and for anything covered by a data use agreement.
The log buffer keeps requests and replies in memory only, and nothing is written to disk unless you save it.

@node Updating the bundled prompt
@chapter Updating the bundled prompt

@cindex prompt file
@cindex sync-prompt

@samp{latex-parser-skill-prompt-file} points at @samp{prompts/transcript-parser.md}.
It may point at an installed @samp{SKILL.md} instead, and the front matter is removed before use.
@samp{make sync-prompt} copies the body of the installed skill into the bundled file.
@samp{make check-prompt} compares the two and fails when they differ.
Both targets read @samp{~/.claude/skills/transcript-parser/SKILL.md} unless @samp{SKILL} names another file, for example @samp{make check-prompt SKILL=path/to/SKILL.md}.

