@node Check the backend
@section Check the backend

@cindex backend!check

The default backend is @samp{claude-code}.
It runs the @samp{claude} program in print mode, so Claude Code must be installed and signed in.
Open a terminal and run the following command to confirm that the program answers.

@example
claude -p "Reply with the word ready."
@end example

If you would rather start with a local model, read the section on switching to a local model first.

@node Open a @LaTeX{} buffer
@section Open a @LaTeX{} buffer

@cindex LaTeX buffer

Visit a new file called @samp{tutorial.tex}.
Emacs opens it in @samp{LaTeX-mode} when AUC@TeX{} is installed, or in the built-in @samp{latex-mode} otherwise.
Type a minimal document skeleton.

@example
\documentclass@{article@}
\begin@{document@}

\end@{document@}
@end example

@node Add the preamble lines
@section Add the preamble lines

@cindex preamble lines
@cindex unchecked macro

The parsed output uses @samp{\index} and a checklist whose items carry the @samp{\unchecked} label.
Press @samp{C-c t p}.
The command inserts @samp{enumitem}, @samp{amssymb}, @samp{makeidx}, @samp{\makeindex} and a definition of @samp{\unchecked} above @samp{\begin@{document@}}.
It skips any line that is already present, so pressing it twice does no harm.

@node Paste the transcript
@section Paste the transcript

@cindex transcript

Put the cursor on the blank line inside the document body.
Insert the sample with @samp{M-x insert-file} and choose @samp{test/fixtures/raw-short.txt}.
The text has contractions, two sound descriptions such as @samp{(coughing)}, and a causal "since".

@node Parse the region
@section Parse the region

@cindex region command

Select the two paragraphs of the transcript and press @samp{C-c t r}.
Emacs asks once per session whether text may leave the computer.
Answer @samp{y}.
The mode line shows @samp{LPS[...]} while the request runs, and Emacs stays responsive.

Behind the scenes four stages run.
Stage 1 expands the unambiguous contractions with the bundled table.
Stage 2 sends the text and the transcript-parser instructions to the model.
Stage 3 checks the reply against the rules of the skill.
Stage 4 sends any rule violation back to the model for one repair pass.

@node Review the preview
@section Review the preview

@cindex preview buffer

A buffer called @samp{*latex-parser-skill-preview*} opens with the result.
The header line lists the keys.

@float Table,org08f97ab
@multitable {aaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Key
@tab Action
@item @samp{C-c C-c}
@tab Accept the result, including any edits you made
@item @samp{C-c C-k}
@tab Reject the result and keep the source text
@item @samp{C-c C-d}
@tab Show a unified diff between the source and the result
@item @samp{C-c C-v}
@tab Run the Stage 3 checks again on the preview
@end multitable
@caption{Keys in the preview buffer.}
@end float

Fix anything you dislike directly in the preview.
Then press @samp{C-c C-c}.
The result replaces the transcript in one undo step, so @samp{C-/} brings the transcript back.

@node Verify the buffer
@section Verify the buffer

@cindex verification report

Press @samp{C-c t v}.
The command runs the Stage 3 checks on the document body and opens @samp{*latex-parser-skill-report*}.
Each entry links to its line.
A clean result reports no violations.
Try typing @samp{don't} somewhere in the body and run the check again to see an entry appear.

@node Switch to a local model
@section Switch to a local model

@cindex local model
@cindex Ollama

Start Ollama and pull a model that follows instructions well.
Then tell the package about it.

@lisp
(setq latex-parser-skill-openai-model "your-model-tag")
@end lisp

Press @samp{C-c t m} and choose @samp{openai-compatible}.
The default URL points at Ollama on this computer, so no question about sending text appears.

@node See chunking at work
@section See chunking at work

@cindex chunking

Insert @samp{test/fixtures/raw-long.txt} into a new buffer in @samp{latex-mode} and run @samp{C-c t b}.
The openai-compatible backend splits input longer than 12000 characters by default.
To watch the split happen on this short file, lower the size first.

@lisp
(setq latex-parser-skill-chunk-sizes '((openai-compatible . 250)))
@end lisp

The mode line counts the chunks.
The result holds one heading group per chunk and a single TODO block at the end.

@node Parse a file without opening it
@section Parse a file without opening it

@cindex file command

Press @samp{C-c t f} and choose @samp{raw-short.txt}.
The package writes @samp{raw-short_parsed.tex} beside it and reports the path.
This matches the naming rule of the transcript-parser skill.

@node Use your own dictation rules
@section Use your own dictation rules

@cindex rule files

Export your dictation replacements to a CSV file of @samp{trigger,replacement} lines.
Add the file to Stage 1.

@lisp
(setq latex-parser-skill-replacement-files '("~/dictation/rules.csv"))
@end lisp

Triggers may hold several words, such as @samp{settle cassin}.
Run @samp{C-c t s} on a region to try Stage 1 alone without calling any model.

@node Read the log
@section Read the log

@cindex log buffer

Press @samp{C-c t l}.
The log lists each request, each reply, the number of Stage 1 replacements, and the timing.
Set @samp{latex-parser-skill-log-content} to nil to keep only sizes and timings.

@node Key Bindings
@chapter Key Bindings

@cindex key bindings
@cindex command map

The package binds no keys by itself, as MELPA asks.
It provides the prefix keymap @samp{latex-parser-skill-command-map} and the transient menu @samp{latex-parser-skill-menu}.
The suggested prefix is @samp{C-c t}.
Emacs reserves @samp{C-c} followed by a plain letter for users, and AUC@TeX{} uses @samp{C-c} followed by a control key, so the two never collide.

