@node Setup with use-package
@section Setup with use-package

@cindex use-package

The following form binds the prefix in both @LaTeX{} modes.
It works in Emacs 28 and later.

@lisp
(use-package latex-parser-skill
  :ensure t
  :defer t
  :init
  (with-eval-after-load 'tex
    (define-key LaTeX-mode-map (kbd "C-c t") 'latex-parser-skill-command-map))
  (with-eval-after-load 'tex-mode
    (define-key latex-mode-map (kbd "C-c t") 'latex-parser-skill-command-map)))
@end lisp

Binding the quoted symbol rather than the keymap value lets the autoload load the package on the first key press.

@node Setup without use-package
@section Setup without use-package

@cindex init file

@lisp
(require 'latex-parser-skill)
(with-eval-after-load 'tex
  (define-key LaTeX-mode-map (kbd "C-c t") latex-parser-skill-command-map))
(with-eval-after-load 'tex-mode
  (define-key latex-mode-map (kbd "C-c t") latex-parser-skill-command-map))
@end lisp

@node If C-c t is taken
@section If C-c t is taken

@cindex alternative prefix

Pick any other @samp{C-c} letter, for example @samp{C-c p}.
Users who prefer one key can bind the transient menu instead of the prefix map.

@lisp
(with-eval-after-load 'tex
  (define-key LaTeX-mode-map (kbd "C-c p") 'latex-parser-skill-menu))
@end lisp

@node Global access to the file command
@section Global access to the file command

@cindex global binding

The file command does not need a @LaTeX{} buffer.
A global binding makes it reachable from Dired or any other buffer.

@lisp
(global-set-key (kbd "C-c T") 'latex-parser-skill-file)
@end lisp

@node Development
@chapter Development

@cindex Makefile
@cindex tests

@float Table,orgd9f2d06
@multitable {aaaaaaaaaaaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem Target
@tab Action
@item @samp{all}
@tab Runs @samp{lint}, @samp{compile}, @samp{test} and @samp{docs}
@item @samp{compile}
@tab Byte-compiles with warnings treated as errors
@item @samp{test}
@tab Runs the offline ERT suite with a fake backend
@item @samp{live-test}
@tab Also runs the live tests against real backends
@item @samp{lint}
@tab Runs checkdoc and package-lint
@item @samp{docs}
@tab Exports this manual to Texinfo and builds the Info file
@item @samp{sync-prompt}
@tab Refreshes the bundled prompt from the installed skill
@item @samp{check-prompt}
@tab Compares the bundled prompt with the installed skill
@item @samp{indent}
@tab Re-indents every Lisp file
@item @samp{clean}
@tab Removes compiled and generated files
@end multitable
@caption{Makefile targets.}
@end float

The live tests run with @samp{LPS_LIVE=1}.
@samp{LPS_LIVE_BACKENDS} names the backends to try, and @samp{LPS_OPENAI_MODEL} names the local model.

